{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/1356219-hack-idea-additional-integrity-analogue-masquerade","id":1356219,"title":"[Hack Idea] Additional Integrity Analogue: Masquerade","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Chronicles of Darkness","Vampire: The Requiem"],"posts":[{"date":"2019-12-20T19:36:55-08:00","id":1356219,"title":"[Hack Idea] Additional Integrity Analogue: Masquerade","content":"In addition to a vampire's Humanity, there's also their personal Masquerade. Like Humanity, it goes from 10 to 0 dots, although it's starting value could vary based on a character's background. It represents how well a vampire conceals their true nature to mortals. Kinda of like Cover in Demon. High Masquerade could give bonuses to Social rolls with their fellow vampires as a form of prestige (&quot;This guy knows how to keep eyes of ya; better listen to them&quot;) or to avoid suspicion for crimes (&quot;Why would they do something so overt? Their history proves otherwise&quot;). Low Masquerade could penalize a vampire's social standing and possibly put a target on their back (&quot;No one's gonna miss 'em. In fact, their death would be celebrated as one less problem-vamp!). It could also be used with vampire hunters. Roll your Masquerade dots to avoid detection.<br /> <br /> Acts that threaten a vampire's Masquerade are known as Breaches. Like Humanity's Breaking Points, Breaches are ranked by dot level and determine the dice pool to roll. Conditions inflicted for failing these rolls would focus on their status in the Domain, such as Notorious or Charged, or with mortals/supernaturals at large, such as Hunted. Example Breaches include:<ul><li>Telling a mortal you're a vampire. Masquerade 7 - 4 dice</li> <li>Killing a mortal. Masquerade 3 - 2 dice</li> <li>Committing an obviously supernatural act in front of a mortal(s), such as breaking down a brick wall with no tools. Masquerade 1 - 1 die</li> </ul><br /> Naturally, specific situations might call for modifiers to dice or the Breach's rating. For example, telling a mortal you're a vampire then laughing it off as a joke might render the Breach to be at Masquerade 10 rather than 7 because it gives the mortal plausible deniability. Killing a mortal by feeding might lower the Breach to Masquerade 2, or even lower if it's in front of other mortals. If a vampire fulfills their Dirge while committing a Breach, it will penalize their roll by -1 for a single point of WP, but -3 for a total WP refresh, as their true nature comes to the fore. Invert these when it comes to Mask, as their lies and excuses aid the Masquerade.<br /> <br /> Like Humanity, a character needs to spend 2 Experiences to gain a Masquerade dot. They work on their cover stories, get rid of the bodies, conceal damning evidence, etc.. To balance out the extra liability Masquerade presents, vampires may have opportunities to gain a dot of either Humanity or Masquerade. These Redemptions would be profound acts that involve some sort of major sacrifice for the vampire. Rushing into a burning building to save a Touchstone could Redeem a Humanity dot. Getting rid of that pesky vampire hunter quietly could Redeem a Masquerade dot.<br /> <br /> The most interesting moments that Masquerade would present are those that pits it against Humanity. What do you do when your Touchstone learns about the big, bad world of vampires? Do you callously silence them permanently, thus ensuring your Masquerade but damning your Humanity? Or do you keep them safe from the horrors, sparing your Humanity but tearing your Masquerade? These moments could present some real dramatic tension as players have something to lose in both cases and forces them to choose.<br /> <br /> However, this is just a half-baked idea. It needs a lot more work to truly flesh it out. That said, what do all you guys think of it?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2992-crapcarp","name":"crapcarp","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2992_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"511"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-12-20T21:07:38-08:00","id":1356230,"title":"","content":"I don't really think Vampire benefits from having an entire mechanical subsystem based around keeping up the act of pretending to be human. It's not a spy game like Demon, and the value of the Masquerade is a very practical matter, revolving around how safe you are from unwanted attention. You could make an argument that telling a mortal you're a vampire is not a meaningful Masquerade breach as long as you're sure the mortal is a trustworthy ally. (Of course, if that alliance then changes, it becomes a problem retroactively.)<br /> <br /> But rejecting the premise isn't helpful if you disagree on that, so if you do find it useful to have Masquerade systems, three things stand out to me:<ul><li>You may want to consider Integrity style breaking points over Humanity style detachment risks. Humanity is designed to inure you to detachments as you degenerate, but that doesn't seem like it fits what Masquerade represents: no matter how ragged your Masquerade runs, you're never going to reach the point where telling a mortal you're a vampire stops being a breach. Rather than tie breaches to specific Masquerade thresholds, you should just tie them to varying degrees of severity that penalize the roll to resist the breach.</li> <li>Conversely, some acts don't make a whole lot of sense as rolled breaches at all. No matter how unlikely you are to succeed on a one-die roll or a chance die, you might succeed and get out of having torn a brick wall to powder with your bare hands in front of a bunch of living acquaintences with their cell phone cameras rolling without your Masquerade suffering. You could address this by considering a model more like a Health track, like Changeling's Clarity or especially Deviant's Stability, where certain acts are just going to damage you, but in-game actions to seal the breach could then heal the damage without having to involve the experience system at all.</li> <li>What does your meter really measure, by the way? It's pulling in two different directions. One is the obvious, measuring how exposed you are to danger and consequences. But the other is social: you make the assumption that one's Masquerade rating would have impact on your relations with other vampires, as much or more as it does with the living. But if it's social, then breaches the domain doesn't find out about shouldn't damage it, and if it's about exposure, it should be damaged by some breaches that the domain won't find out about and therefore shouldn't punish you for. Is this a reputation meter or a spy meter?</li> </ul>","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/522-stupid-loserman","name":"Stupid Loserman","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar522_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1824"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-12-21T03:12:43-08:00","id":1356268,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Stupid Loserman</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/1356219-hack-idea-additional-integrity-analogue-masquerade?p=1356230#post1356230\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I don't really think Vampire benefits from having an entire mechanical subsystem based around keeping up the act of pretending to be human. It's not a spy game like Demon, and the value of the Masquerade is a very practical matter, revolving around how safe you are from unwanted attention. You could make an argument that telling a mortal you're a vampire is not a meaningful Masquerade breach as long as you're sure the mortal is a trustworthy ally. (Of course, if that alliance then changes, it becomes a problem retroactively.)</div> </div> </div> </div>I disagree. The Masquerade is the First Tradition for a reason. It may not be the primal focus like in, well, Vampire: the Masquerade, but it still plays a big part in Requiem. Unlike werewolves, mages, and other supernaturals, vampires don't get the luxury of having the universe do the heavy lifting of keeping them hidden from the ignorant masses. They have to ensure they remain hidden from their food themselves. Masquerade as an additional Integrity analogue gives mechanical weight to the issue.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Stupid Loserman</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/1356219-hack-idea-additional-integrity-analogue-masquerade?p=1356230#post1356230\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">You may want to consider Integrity style breaking points over Humanity style detachment risks. Humanity is designed to inure you to detachments as you degenerate, but that doesn't seem like it fits what Masquerade represents: no matter how ragged your Masquerade runs, you're never going to reach the point where telling a mortal you're a vampire stops being a breach. Rather than tie breaches to specific Masquerade thresholds, you should just tie them to varying degrees of severity that penalize the roll to resist the breach.<br /> <br /> Conversely, some acts don't make a whole lot of sense as rolled breaches at all. No matter how unlikely you are to succeed on a one-die roll or a chance die, you might succeed and get out of having torn a brick wall to powder with your bare hands in front of a bunch of living acquaintences with their cell phone cameras rolling without your Masquerade suffering. You could address this by considering a model more like a Health track, like Changeling's Clarity or especially Deviant's Stability, where certain acts are just going to damage you, but in-game actions to seal the breach could then heal the damage without having to involve the experience system at all.</div> </div> </div> </div>That's actually a good point. It'd give you two dimensions with which to determine the severity of a Breach: amount and level of damage. Each Breach could have a base level and amount of damage, and rolls can determine extra damage on top. Circumstances can either modify the roll or perhaps even upgrade the damage level. Tearing down a brick wall with your bare hands will inflict a lot of lethal (for lack of a more suitable term) damage to your Masquerade boxes in and of itself. Doing in front of a mortal you know might add a bonus to the roll to determine extra damage. Doing it in front of a full crowd of mortals with phone cameras might upgrade the damage to aggravated.<br /> <br /> Just spitballin' here, by the way. Implementing all of these possibilities has a good chance of over-complicating the system. I just like to come up with a lot of bits to initially work with and then trim the ones that aren't working well afterwards.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Stupid Loserman</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/1356219-hack-idea-additional-integrity-analogue-masquerade?p=1356230#post1356230\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">What does your meter really measure, by the way? It's pulling in two different directions. One is the obvious, measuring how exposed you are to danger and consequences. But the other is social: you make the assumption that one's Masquerade rating would have impact on your relations with other vampires, as much or more as it does with the living. But if it's social, then breaches the domain doesn't find out about shouldn't damage it, and if it's about exposure, it should be damaged by some breaches that the domain won't find out about and therefore shouldn't punish you for. Is this a reputation meter or a spy meter?</div> </div> </div> </div>To be honest, I only mentioned the social modifiers as a means of relating it to vamp politics as a tangential function rather than a core one. Sorry if I gave the wrong impression on that.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2992-crapcarp","name":"crapcarp","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2992_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"511"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-12-21T05:17:57-08:00","id":1356277,"title":"","content":"Have you considered using the changeling 2e clarity as an analogue instead of cover?<br /> Maybe treating Masquerade as health would work better for what you are trying to do.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Completed campaign: Scion 2nd Edition. Les L\u00e9gendes Currently playing: Being a dad for a 3 year old daughter and a 2 years old son and now a beautiful new baby.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11312-maitrecorbo","name":"Maitrecorbo","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11312_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"719"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}